\section{A Location Counter Per Input Section}

A label does not denote a position in the source file or a byte offset in the eventual executable. In ordinary relocatable ELF it denotes a position in one input section. John R. Levine's Chapter~3 explains section-relative symbol values and distinguishes initialized sections from \texttt{NOBITS} storage \cite[chapter~3]{levine2000linkers}. A typical assembler therefore keeps separate location counters. Switching to a section resumes that section's counter, and updating it does not move a label in any other section. Reserving eight bytes in \texttt{.bss} increases its logical size without adding eight bytes of initialized section contents to the object.

For an assembler with fixed-size instructions and directives, the location counter measures the size laid out so far in that section. A label records the current counter without advancing it. A byte directive advances it by one; an alignment directive advances it by enough padding to reach the specified boundary. With positive alignment $a$ and section offset $p$, unrestricted padding is $(-p)\bmod a$, taking the nonnegative residue. The object also needs a section alignment requirement compatible with those internal offsets. Otherwise a linker could place the section at a base that invalidates its assumed alignment.

\section{Assign Positions Before Evaluating Uses}

Chapter~5 of Ed Jorgensen's \emph{x86-64 Assembly Language Programming with Ubuntu} motivates two-pass assembly with a forward branch. In a simple implementation, the first pass parses and expands source, chooses fixed sizes, and records labels and declarations. The second uses the completed table to fill instruction and data fields and emit the object. A parser can read the source once and the second pass can traverse buffered fragments instead of reopening it. This retains the distinction between discovering positions and relying on positions. Levine makes the analogous distinction for linking in Chapter~1, and in Chapter~4 describes discovering the sizes that determine later placements. His linker pass assigns positions across input modules; an assembler pass works within each input section.

Keep a symbol's name, location, and binding separate. An ordinary defined symbol can store a section and an offset, while an absolute equate needs no section base. A declaration with \texttt{.globl} sets global binding but by itself attaches no location; ELF visibility is a separate attribute. For ordinary labels, a second definition of the same symbol is a diagnostic, not an instruction to update every older reference. Special directives that deliberately reassign equates require their own rules.

