\section{Resume a Section and Settle a Forward Difference}

Save this GNU \texttt{as} input as \texttt{sample.s}. The forward expression asks for a byte count, and the field that will hold that count is always four bytes wide.

\begin{verbatim}
.section .rodata
.globl sample
.type sample, @object
sample:
.Lbegin:
    .byte 0x31
    .balign 4, 0
    # Reserve four bytes now; the endpoint comes later.
    .long .Lend - .Lbegin
.bss
    .balign 8
.globl scratch
.type scratch, @object
scratch:
    # BSS advances its own counter without file payload.
    .zero 8
.size scratch, . - scratch
.section .rodata
    .short 0x0201
.Lend:
.size sample, . - sample
\end{verbatim}

The first byte puts the read-only counter at one. Alignment adds three bytes, and the four-byte field ends at offset eight. The switch to \texttt{.bss} lays out \texttt{scratch} at its own offset zero and increases only that section's size. Back in \texttt{.rodata}, the two-byte value starts at eight, so the endpoint is ten. On the second pass the forward difference is $10-0=10$, written at offset four as \texttt{0A 00 00 00}, with no object relocation. The final data bytes at offsets eight and nine are \texttt{01 02}.

\Cref{fig:visual-282} follows the two counters through both section switches.

