\section{Check Both Layout and Emission}

Assemble the object, then inspect its headers, symbols, data, and relocations:

\begin{verbatim}
as --64 -o sample.o sample.s
readelf -SWs sample.o
readelf -x .rodata -r sample.o
\end{verbatim}

Expect \texttt{sample} and \texttt{scratch} both to have a value of zero, but different section indices and sizes of ten and eight, respectively. The \texttt{.bss} header reports size eight and type \texttt{NOBITS}. Increasing \verb|.zero 8| to \verb|.zero 24| should change the \texttt{.bss} size and the size of \texttt{scratch}, not the other section's bytes. This checks that separate counters actually remain separate.

At emission, every ordinary label should equal the offset obtained by replaying the laid-out fragments of its own section. The replay must use the same sizes and padding as layout. A forward reference in a fixed-width field has a harmless dependency on a later offset. A directive that sets the size of a fragment from a forward distance including that very fragment may not have a unique solution. A fixed-layout two-pass assembler should reject such a dependency unless it has explicit rules for solving it. Variable-size instruction forms require a different treatment, not a second pass that silently moves labels.

An assembler listing can help diagnose this distinction, but compare it with the symbol table before relying on a printed number. In this example a listing could print two zero offsets on different source lines without contradiction. The complete position is a pair, not an integer. Only after a linker places each input section can an ordinary symbol acquire an address in the linked image.
